-- title:
--   Divisorial Weil pairing datum on Pic⁰[n] over ̄ K
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and $F$ a field that is a $K$-algebra, and assume there is an element $x \in F$ transcendental over $K$ such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Assume further `IsCurveOver K F`: every nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor, i.e. a finitely supported integer-valued function on the set of places of $F/K$ (valuation subrings of $F$ containing the image of $K$, distinct from $F$ itself and principal ideal rings) agreeing with $v \mapsto \operatorname{ord}_v f$ and of degree zero; each residue field of a place is a finite $K$-module; and $\Omega_{F/K}$ is free of rank one over $F$. Let $n$ be a nonzero natural number. Then the type `DivisorialWeilPairingData K F n` is nonempty: there exist a function $\mathrm{pair}$ on pairs of $n$-torsion classes of $\operatorname{Pic}^0(F/K)$ (degree-zero divisors modulo principal ones) with values in $K$ such that, first, for every Weil datum — divisors $D_1, D_2$ and nonzero $f_1, f_2$ with $\operatorname{ord}_v f_i = n\,D_i(v)$ for all $v$, with $D_1(v) = 0$ or $D_2(v) = 0$ for every $v$, and with every place in the support of $D_1$ or $D_2$ rational (its residue field generated by the image of $K$) — the value of $\mathrm{pair}$ on the classes of $D_1$ and $D_2$ equals $\mathrm{evalFun}(f_1, D_2)/\mathrm{evalFun}(f_2, D_1)$; and second, every $n$-torsion class is represented by a degree-zero divisor whose support consists of rational places avoiding any prescribed finite set of places.
--
--   This is the construction of the Weil pairing $e_n$ on the $n$-torsion of the Jacobian of a curve, in divisorial form $e_n(x,y) = f_x(D_y)/f_y(D_x)$ computed on disjoint representatives, with no restriction on the characteristic of the algebraically closed base field or on $n$ (perfectness, not asserted here, would require $n$ prime to the characteristic). It supplies the pairing used in the local analysis of the $J_H$ Néron model at $p$, where toric and finite parts of the component group are shown to annihilate one another.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Pic0_nonempty_divisorialWeilPairingData_of_isAlgClosed.lean

